8f0b8c99-114d-45ad-b069-df1c7893701a	Nashville, TN: STR Permit Requirements & Music City Market	nashville-tn-str-permit-requirements	STR Regulations	National	USA	Complete guide to Nashville's complex STR permit system, owner-occupied vs non-owner-occupied requirements, and market performance.	Nashville, TN: Navigating STR and Co-Living Regulations for the Rental Arbitrage Investor\n\nIntroduction: Music City's Complex STR Landscape\n\nNashville, Tennessee, offers rental arbitrage investors access to one of the nation's hottest tourism markets. As the country music capital with a thriving entertainment, healthcare, and tech economy, Nashville delivers strong year-round demand. However, the city's complex STR permit system, with distinct rules for owner-occupied versus non-owner-occupied properties, creates significant regulatory hurdles. Understanding Nashville's permit requirements, zoning restrictions, and the path to compliance is critical for investors seeking to capitalize on this high-demand market.\n\nI. Short-Term Rental (STR) License and Regulations\n\nTwo-Tier Permit System\nNashville's STR framework divides operations based on owner occupancy:\n\nOwner-Occupied STR Permit\n• Definition: Property where owner resides as primary residence; may rent entire home or individual rooms.\n• Permit Fee: $50 initial application + $50 annual renewal\n• Zoning: Permitted in all residential zoning districts.\n• Rental Cap: No limit on rental days per year.\n• Occupancy Limit: Maximum 2 guests per bedroom + 4 additional guests.\n• Application: Apply through Nashville Metro Codes Department; includes property inspection.\n\nNon-Owner-Occupied STR Permit\n• Definition: Entire property rented as STR; owner does not reside on-site.\n• Permit Fee: $200 initial application + $200 annual renewal\n• Zoning Restrictions: Only permitted in specific zoning districts (RS, RM, OR, ORI, MUL, MUN, MUI, DTC, SCR, SCN, CF, IR, IWD).\n• Permit Cap: Nashville has capped the total number of non-owner-occupied permits; waitlist may apply.\n• Distance Requirements: Must be at least 100 feet from another non-owner-occupied STR.\n• Rental Cap: No limit on rental days per year (if permit obtained).\n• Occupancy Limit: Maximum 2 guests per bedroom + 4 additional guests.\n• Rental Arbitrage Applicability: MODERATE - Permit caps and zoning restrictions limit availability.\n\nKey Compliance Requirements (All Permits)\n• Business License: Required annual business license from Metro Nashville.\n• Tax Collection: 6.25% Hotel Occupancy Tax + 7% state sales tax = 13.25% total.\n• Safety Standards: Smoke detectors, CO detectors, fire extinguishers required per Metro fire code.\n• Parking: Must provide 1 off-street parking space per bedroom.\n• Good Neighbor Policy: Must provide 24/7 contact information and respond to complaints promptly.\n• Inspection: Properties subject to inspection by Metro Codes before permit issuance and renewal.\n\nRental Arbitrage Perspective\nNashville is a Moderate-Opportunity, High-Complexity market for rental arbitrage STRs.\n• Investor Challenge: Non-owner-occupied permit caps create artificial scarcity; securing permits requires timing and persistence.\n• Key Strategy: Focus on properties in permitted zoning districts; maintain strong relationships with landlords who understand STR regulations.\n• Risk Mitigation: Ensure lease explicitly permits STR subletting; verify zoning compliance before signing lease.\n\nII. Co-Living License and Regulations\n\nCo-living in Nashville operates under Tennessee state law with local zoning overlay:\n\n1. Standard Co-Living (Rental-by-the-Room)\n• Model: Individual room rentals under separate long-term leases (30+ days) without care services.\n• License Requirement: No special license required; operates under standard landlord-tenant law.\n• Zoning: Must comply with Nashville's residential occupancy limits (typically 4-5 unrelated adults).\n• Arbitrage Perspective: Low regulatory friction; primary focus is landlord approval and professional management.\n\n2. Specialized Co-Living (Group Homes, Sober Living)\n• Model: Facilities providing treatment, recovery, or specialized support services.\n• License Requirement: Must be licensed by Tennessee Department of Mental Health and Substance Abuse Services (TDMHSAS).\n• Facility Types:\n  - Residential Treatment Facilities: Requires TDMHSAS license, clinical oversight\n  - Sober Living Homes: May require certification depending on services offered\n  - Adult Care Homes: Requires state license and local zoning approval\n• Zoning: Nashville has specific zoning categories for community residences; separation requirements apply.\n• Arbitrage Perspective: High regulatory friction; requires significant operational expertise and ongoing compliance.\n\nIII. Performance Metrics for Rental Arbitrage\n\nShort-Term Rental (STR) Performance:\n• Average Daily Rate (ADR): $191-$221\n• Occupancy Rate: 51-59%\n• Estimated Annual Revenue: $38,000-$50,000 per property\n• Peak Seasons: CMA Fest (June), NFL season (September-January), New Year's Eve\n• Top Neighborhoods: Downtown/Broadway, The Gulch, East Nashville, Germantown, 12 South\n\nCo-Living (Per Room, Monthly) Performance:\n• Standard Co-Living: $650-$950 per room/month\n• Specialized Co-Living: $1,000-$1,600 per room/month (includes services)\n• Typical Property Size: 4-6 bedrooms\n• Estimated Annual Revenue: $31,200-$68,400 per property (4 rooms)\n\nAccess Your Place has successfully helped clients launch 130+ properties in Nashville over the past 5 years, navigating the permit cap system and zoning restrictions.\n\nReady to launch in Nashville? Book a discovery call: https://calendly.com/investyourplaces\n	Nashville TN STR permits: Owner-occupied vs non-owner-occupied. Metro Codes requirements. ADR $191-221, 51-59% occupancy.	["Nashville STR", "Nashville Airbnb permit", "Nashville rental arbitrage", "Tennessee vacation rental"]	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	published	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2025-12-01 14