ward licensing process\n• Key Strategy: Focus on properties near Old Town, Nob Hill, and UNM for highest demand\n• Risk Mitigation: Ensure lease explicitly permits STR subletting; verify zoning compliance\n\nII. Co-Living License and Regulations\n\nCo-living in Albuquerque operates under standard residential rental law:\n\n1. Standard Co-Living (Room Rentals)\n• Model: Individual room rentals under separate 30+ day leases\n• License Requirement: No special license; standard landlord-tenant law applies\n• Zoning: Must comply with occupancy limits (typically 4-5 unrelated adults)\n• Arbitrage Perspective: Low regulatory friction; focus on landlord approval\n\n2. Specialized Co-Living (Group Homes)\n• Model: Facilities providing treatment or support services\n• License Requirement: New Mexico Department of Health licensing required\n• Facility Types: Behavioral health residential, sober living, adult care homes\n• Arbitrage Perspective: High regulatory friction; requires operational expertise\n\nIII. Performance Metrics for Rental Arbitrage\n\nShort-Term Rental Performance:\n• Average Daily Rate (ADR): $95-$135\n• Occupancy Rate: 52-61%\n• Estimated Annual Revenue: $18,000-$30,000 per property\n• Peak Seasons: Balloon Fiesta (October), summer tourism (June-August)\n• Top Neighborhoods: Old Town, Nob Hill, Downtown, University area\n\nCo-Living Performance (Per Room, Monthly):\n• Standard Co-Living: $450-$650 per room/month\n• Specialized Co-Living: $800-$1,200 per room/month\n• Typical Property Size: 4-6 bedrooms\n• Estimated Annual Revenue: $21,600-$46,800 per property (4 rooms)\n\nAccess Your Place has helped clients launch 15+ properties in Albuquerque, capitalizing on the Balloon Fiesta surge and year-round demand.\n\nReady to launch in Albuquerque? Book a discovery call: https://calendly.com/investyourplaces\n	Albuquerque NM STR regulations: Transient Lodging license, 7.875% tax. ADR $95-135, 52-61% occupancy. Balloon Fiesta surge demand.	["Albuquerque STR", "Albu