870a198f-5cdf-4d43-911c-963123a004fb	Orlando/Kissimmee, FL: Theme Park Capital STR Guide	orlando-kissimmee-fl-str-guide	STR Regulations	National	USA	Navigate Orlando and Kissimmee STR regulations with our complete guide to Florida DBPR licensing and theme park market opportunities.	Orlando/Kissimmee, FL: Navigating STR and Co-Living Regulations for the Rental Arbitrage Investor\n\nIntroduction: The Theme Park Capital Investment Opportunity\n\nOrlando and Kissimmee represent one of the most lucrative STR markets in the United States, driven by Disney World, Universal Studios, and year-round tourism exceeding 75 million annual visitors. For rental arbitrage investors, this market offers exceptional demand fundamentals with straightforward regulatory compliance under Florida state law.\n\nI. Short-Term Rental (STR) License and Regulations\n\nState and Local Regulatory Framework\nBoth Orlando and Kissimmee STR operators must comply with Florida DBPR licensing. Kissimmee (Osceola County) has additional local requirements, while Orlando maintains a more streamlined approach.\n\nLicense and Permit Requirements\n• State DBPR License: Required for all vacation rentals\n  - Single-Unit License: $100 annually\n  - Multi-Unit License: $200 annually for 2+ properties\n• Orlando-Specific Requirements:\n  - Business Tax Receipt: $50-$75 annually\n  - No additional city STR permit required\n  - Tax collection: 6% state sales tax + 6% Orange County Tourist Development Tax = 12% total\n• Kissimmee/Osceola County Requirements:\n  - Tourist Development Tax Certificate required\n  - Business Tax Receipt: $50-$100 annually\n  - Tax collection: 6% state + 6% Osceola Tourist Tax = 12% total\n  - Stricter enforcement in resort corridor areas\n\nRental Arbitrage Perspective: Exceptional Opportunity Market\nOrlando/Kissimmee is an Elite-Tier, High-Opportunity market for rental arbitrage.\n• Investor Leverage: Extremely high tourism demand makes landlord approval easier when presenting professional revenue projections\n• Market Strength: Year-round 70%+ occupancy rates with peak seasons (summer, holidays, spring break) driving premium rates\n• Primary Focus: Properties within 15 minutes of Disney/Universal command significant rate premiums\n\nII. Co-Living License and Regulations\n\n1. Standard Co-Living (Rental-by-the-Room)\n• Model: Individual rooms under separate 30+ day leases\n• License Requirement: None. Operates under Florida Residential Landlord-Tenant Act\n• Arbitrage Perspective: Moderate opportunity. STR performance typically exceeds co-living returns in this market due to exceptional tourism demand\n• Best Use Cases: Properties near UCF, downtown Orlando, or medical district\n\n2. Specialized Co-Living (Group Homes)\n• License Requirement: Florida DCF or AHCA licensing required\n• Regulatory Friction: High complexity\n• Arbitrage Perspective: Limited appeal given superior STR returns\n\nIII. Performance Metrics for Rental Arbitrage\n\nShort-Term Rental (STR) Performance:\n• Average Daily Rate (ADR): $145-$285\n• Occupancy Rate: 70-82%\n• Estimated Annual Revenue: $37,000-$85,000 per property\n• Top Areas: Kissimmee resort corridor, Dr. Phillips, Lake Nona, Celebration, Champions Gate\n\nCo-Living (Per Room, Monthly) Performance:\n• Standard Co-Living: $600-$850 per room/month\n• Typical Property Size: 4-6 bedrooms\n• Estimated Annual Revenue: $28,800-$61,200 per property (4 rooms)\n\nAccess Your Place has successfully helped clients launch 300+ properties in the Orlando/Kissimmee market, making it our highest-volume market.\n\nReady to launch in Orlando? Book a discovery call: https://calendly.com/investyourplaces\n	Orlando Kissimmee FL STR: DBPR license, 12% tax. ADR $145-285, 70-82% occupancy. Elite theme park market.	["Orlando STR", "Kissimmee Airbnb", "Disney vacation rental", "Florida theme park rental"]	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	published	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2025-12-01 14:10:30.012675+00	2025-12-01 14:10:30.012675+00	2025-12-01 14:10:29.966+00	\N
